CAT = "casfaBleed"; SEG = "BLD"
Q = [
("What is the first step to control severe bleeding from a wound?",
 "Apply firm, direct pressure", ["Raise the limb above the head", "Wash the wound with water", "Apply a tourniquet first"],
 "Firm, direct pressure on the wound with a dressing or your gloved hand is the first and most effective way to stop bleeding."),
("Blood soaks through the dressing on a wound. What should you do?",
 "Add more dressings on top", ["Remove it and start over", "Loosen the pressure", "Run water over the wound"],
 "Removing a soaked dressing can pull away clots. Put more dressings on top and keep pressing firmly."),
("When should a tourniquet be used?",
 "For life-threatening limb bleeding", ["For any cut on an arm or leg", "For a heavy nosebleed that won't stop", "For bleeding from the scalp"],
 "A tourniquet is for severe, life-threatening bleeding from an arm or leg that direct pressure cannot control, or when you cannot reach the wound."),
("Where should a tourniquet be placed?",
 "5 to 7.5 cm above the wound", ["Directly on top of the wound", "Below the wound, nearer the hand", "Over the nearest joint"],
 "Place it on the limb about 5 to 7.5 cm above the wound, not over a joint, and tighten until the bleeding stops."),
("Once a tourniquet is on, what should you do?",
 "Note the time and leave it on", ["Loosen it every 10 minutes", "Remove it after 30 minutes", "Cover it so no one sees it"],
 "Only medical staff should remove a tourniquet. Write down the time it was applied so they know how long blood flow has been cut off."),
("A tourniquet is tightened and the bleeding has slowed but not stopped. What should you do?",
 "Tighten it further", ["Loosen it slightly", "Remove it and apply pressure", "Add a second dressing under it"],
 "A tourniquet that is too loose can make bleeding worse by blocking the veins but not the arteries. Tighten it until the bleeding stops; it will be painful."),
("A piece of glass is stuck deep in a person's forearm. What should you do?",
 "Leave it in and pad around it", ["Pull it out quickly and press", "Push it in to steady it", "Wash it out with water"],
 "The object may be plugging the wound. Pull it out and the bleeding can get worse. Build up dressings around it and bandage them in place."),
("Someone has a nosebleed. How should they sit?",
 "Leaning forward", ["Tilted back, looking up", "Lying flat on their back", "Head between their knees"],
 "Leaning forward stops blood running down the throat, which can cause vomiting. Pinch the soft part of the nose."),
("How long should a person pinch their nose to stop a nosebleed before checking it?",
 "About 10 minutes", ["About 30 seconds", "About 1 minute", "About 1 hour"],
 "Pinch the soft part of the nose firmly for about 10 minutes, then release slowly. Seek medical help if it keeps bleeding for over 20 minutes."),
("How should you care for an amputated finger?",
 "Wrap it, bag it and keep it cool", ["Put it straight on bare ice", "Soak it in a cup of water", "Wash it with soap and alcohol"],
 "Wrap the part in sterile gauze, seal it in a plastic bag and keep the bag cool on ice. Direct contact with ice or water can damage the tissue."),
("How should you clean a minor scrape?",
 "Rinse it under running water", ["Scrub it with rubbing alcohol", "Cover it with butter", "Leave the dirt for the doctor"],
 "Running water washes out dirt and germs. Clean the area around it with mild soap, then cover it with a sterile dressing."),
("Which sign suggests a wound has become infected?",
 "Increasing redness and warmth", ["A thin scab forming on top", "Mild itching as it heals", "Pale pink new skin at the edges"],
 "Redness, warmth, swelling, pus, red streaks or fever a day or more after the injury suggest infection and need medical care."),
("Which wound carries a risk of tetanus?",
 "A deep, dirty puncture", ["A small, clean paper cut", "A sunburn on the arm", "A bruise from a fall"],
 "Deep, dirty puncture wounds give tetanus bacteria a place to grow. The person should check that their tetanus shot is up to date."),
("What is the difference between a dressing and a bandage?",
 "A dressing touches the wound", ["A bandage touches the wound", "A dressing is always elastic", "A bandage is always sterile"],
 "A dressing is the sterile pad placed directly on the wound. A bandage holds the dressing in place and can add pressure."),
("After bandaging an arm, how do you check it is not too tight?",
 "Check the fingers for colour", ["Ask them to lift the arm high", "Squeeze the bandage firmly", "Check the pulse in the neck"],
 "Cold, pale or bluish fingers, numbness or tingling mean circulation is cut off. Loosen the bandage if so."),
("Which sign may point to internal bleeding after a hard blow to the abdomen?",
 "A tender, rigid belly", ["A slow, strong pulse", "Warm, flushed skin", "Better colour over time"],
 "Bruising, a tender or rigid abdomen, vomiting or coughing up blood and signs of shock suggest internal bleeding. Call EMS."),
("What is shock, in first aid terms?",
 "Too little blood flow to organs", ["A short fright after an injury", "An injury from an electric current", "A reaction to cold water only"],
 "Shock is a failure of the circulation to supply enough oxygen-rich blood to the body. It can follow blood loss, burns, infection or allergy, and can be fatal."),
("Which set of signs points to shock?",
 "Pale, clammy skin, fast pulse", ["Red, dry skin, slow pulse", "Slow breathing and a warm face", "Clear thinking and strong pulses"],
 "As the body diverts blood to vital organs, the skin turns pale, cool and clammy and the pulse and breathing speed up. Restlessness and thirst are common."),
("How should you care for someone showing signs of shock?",
 "Lay them down, keep them warm", ["Sit them up and give them food", "Walk them around to stay alert", "Cool them down with cold water"],
 "Lay the person down, keep them warm, reassure them, care for the cause and call EMS. Stay with them and monitor breathing."),
("A person in shock asks for a drink of water. What should you do?",
 "Give no drink; wet the lips", ["Give a large glass of water", "Give a cup of sweet tea", "Give a small shot of alcohol"],
 "The person may need surgery and could vomit, so give nothing to eat or drink. You can moisten the lips if they are thirsty."),
("A deep cut on the palm is bleeding heavily. Which helps most along with direct pressure?",
 "Having the person lie down", ["Wiggling the fingers often", "Running hot water over it", "Leaving the wound uncovered"],
 "Direct pressure is the key step. Having the person rest and lie down helps prevent fainting and slows the progress of shock."),
("Which wound needs medical attention for possible stitches?",
 "A gaping cut that will not close", ["A shallow graze on the knee", "A small blister on the back of a heel", "A light paper cut on a finger"],
 "Wounds that gape, are deep, bleed heavily or are on the face or a joint often need stitches. They are best closed within a few hours."),
("A person has a bad cut and is on blood thinners. What should you expect?",
 "The bleeding may be harder to stop", ["The bleeding will stop much faster", "The wound cannot get infected", "No dressing will be needed"],
 "Anticoagulant medicines stop blood from clotting as easily. Keep firm pressure longer and get medical help sooner."),
("What colour is bleeding from an artery usually, and how does it flow?",
 "Bright red, spurting", ["Dark red, steady flow", "Pink, slow oozing", "Brown, in small drops"],
 "Arterial blood is oxygen-rich and under pressure, so it is bright red and spurts with each heartbeat. It needs urgent, firm pressure."),
("Blood is coming from a person's ear after a head injury. What should you do?",
 "Cover it loosely; do not plug it", ["Pack the ear tightly with gauze", "Rinse the ear with water", "Tilt the head to drain the other side"],
 "Blood or clear fluid from the ear after a head injury may mean a skull fracture. Cover it loosely, keep the head still and call EMS."),
("A tooth has been knocked out. How should you transport it?",
 "In milk, held by the crown", ["Wrapped in dry tissue", "Scrubbed clean in tap water", "Held by the root in a pocket"],
 "Hold the tooth by the crown, not the root, and keep it moist in milk or a tooth-saving solution. See a dentist within about 30 minutes."),
("A splinter is in the surface of a finger. How should you remove it?",
 "With clean tweezers along its line", ["Squeeze it out with your nails", "Cut deep around it with a knife", "Burn it out with a match"],
 "Grip the end with clean tweezers and pull it out at the same angle it went in, then wash the area. Deep splinters need medical care."),
("Why are disposable gloves worn when caring for a bleeding wound?",
 "To block infection both ways", ["To keep the wound warm", "To grip the dressing better", "To help the blood clot faster"],
 "Gloves protect you from bloodborne disease and protect the wound from germs on your hands."),
]
